        {"question": "A contractor decides a tank entry does NOT need a permit because 'we do this all the time and it's always been fine.' The tank previously contained diesel fuel, was drained and steam-cleaned 2 days ago. No air monitoring is done. Two workers enter to repair a valve. What is required before such a decision?", "options": ["A. The contractor's experience is sufficient — they know the tank is safe", "B. A competent person must evaluate the space using the criteria in 1926.1203: (1) is it a confined space? (2) does it contain or potentially contain a hazardous atmosphere, engulfment hazard, configuration trap, or other serious hazard? The previous diesel fuel content creates a presumption of atmospheric hazard — air monitoring is mandatory before and during entry to demonstrate the space is safe", "C. No requirements — tank entries are routine in construction", "D. The contractor needs a hot work permit only"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "1926.1203(a): before entry, the employer must determine if the space is a permit-required confined space. 1926.1203(d)(1): air monitoring must be done before entry. A diesel tank, even steam-cleaned, can have: (1) residual vapors from sludge/pockets not reached by cleaning, (2) oxygen deficiency if rust consumes O₂ faster than the manhole admits air, (3) H₂S or CO from residual fuel decomposition. The contractor's anecdotal 'always been fine' does not substitute for testing. 1926.1203(g) allows reclassifying a permit space to non-permit IF: no actual or potential atmospheric hazards exist AND all other hazards are eliminated — but this requires testing to prove, not assumption. A diesel tank is a permit-required confined space until air monitoring PROVES it is safe."},
        {"question": "An entry supervisor records pre-entry atmospheric test results on the permit, signs it, and workers enter. The permit does NOT include: the rescue service contact information, the communication method between attendant and entrants, or the list of equipment (harnesses, retrieval line, ventilation). Is the permit compliant?", "options": ["A. Yes — the permit just needs test results and a signature", "B. No — 1926.1204(c) specifies the minimum permit contents: (1) space identification, (2) purpose of entry, (3) date and authorized duration, (4) authorized entrants, attendants, and entry supervisor names, (5) hazards of the space, (6) measures to isolate the space (LOTO, blanking), (7) acceptable entry conditions (test results), (8) rescue and emergency services contact, (9) communication procedures, (10) equipment list, (11) any other necessary information, (12) any additional permits (hot work)", "C. Yes — additional info is optional", "D. The permit is valid as long as workers know what to do in an emergency"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "1926.1204(c): the entry permit must contain (at minimum) all 12 listed items. Missing rescue service info means if an emergency occurs, no one knows who to call or whether a rescue team is on standby. Missing communication procedures means the attendant may not know how to contact entrants (radio channel, rope pulls, voice). Missing equipment list means critical safety gear may not be present (and indeed, retrieval equipment is missing). An incomplete permit is non-compliant — the permit is not just a formality, it's a safety checklist that verifies all safeguards are in place before entry."},
        {"question": "A worker enters a manhole that has been closed for 6 months. No air monitoring. Within 30 seconds, the worker collapses. A co-worker at the surface sees this and immediately climbs down the ladder to rescue them. The co-worker also collapses. What just happened?", "options": ["A. Both workers had simultaneous heart attacks — a tragic coincidence", "B. This is the classic 'confined space double fatality' scenario: an oxygen-deficient or toxic atmosphere incapacitates the first entrant. The would-be rescuer, untrained and unequipped, enters the same atmosphere and becomes the second victim. Over 60% of confined space fatalities are would-be rescuers", "C. The ladder broke, injuring both workers", "D. The workers were overcome by sewer gas (smells bad but isn't dangerous)"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "1926.1209(a): the employer must develop and implement procedures for summoning rescue and emergency services. 1926.1209(c): retrieval systems must be used unless they would increase the overall risk. Over 60% of confined space fatalities are would-be rescuers — the phenomenon is so well-documented it has its own name. A manhole closed for 6 months likely has: (1) O₂ consumed by rust/organic decomposition (well below 19.5%), (2) accumulated H₂S from stagnant water (paralyzes olfactory nerve at high concentrations — you can't smell it when it's deadly), (3) CO or methane. The correct rescue procedure: NEVER enter without supplied-air respirator and backup team. Use non-entry rescue (retrieval line and winch to pull the worker out) if possible. Call 911. This scenario repeats itself dozens of times each year."},
